EIDAToolkit iOS SDK
===================

Version: 3.1.6
Device target: iOS arm64 (no simulator slice)
Minimum deployment target: iOS 12.0

Directory layout
----------------
  lib/
    objective-c/IDCardToolkit.framework  Objective-C dynamic framework
    swift/IDCardToolkit.framework        Swift dynamic framework
    swift/ToolkitFace/                   Face authentication Swift source module
  plugins/
    Feitian/Feitian.framework            Feitian iR301 smart card plugin
    Grabba/Grabba.framework              Grabba reader plugin (binary-only)
    NFC/iOSNfcPlugin.framework           NFC reader plugin
    Tactivo/libTactivo.framework         Tactivo reader plugin (binary-only)
  ext/
    openssl/
      include/iOS/openssl/               OpenSSL 1.1.x headers (58 files)
      lib/iOS/                           libcrypto.a + libssl.a (arm64 device)
    xmlsec/
      include/MacOSX/{libxml,libxslt,libexslt}/
      lib/iOS/                           libxmlsec1.a + libxmlsec1-openssl.a
  samples/                                Swift + ObjC sample apps
  doc/                                    Developer PDFs

Typical usage (Xcode project)
-----------------------------
Most apps use lib/objective-c/IDCardToolkit.framework or
lib/swift/IDCardToolkit.framework directly. These frameworks are
**self-contained** — OpenSSL and xmlsec are statically linked in. Add the
framework to your target and you're done.

Advanced: linking libxmlsec.a directly
--------------------------------------
If you build a custom iOS tool that reuses ext/xmlsec/lib/iOS/libxmlsec1.a
without going through the framework, you must also link against the OpenSSL
static libs shipped in ext/openssl/lib/iOS/. Otherwise the linker will fail
with undefined symbols for every OpenSSL function referenced by xmlsec
(EVP_*, X509_*, PKCS12_*, RSA_*, EC_*, etc.).

  OTHER_LDFLAGS = -L$(SRCROOT)/.../ext/openssl/lib/iOS \\
                  -L$(SRCROOT)/.../ext/xmlsec/lib/iOS \\
                  -lxmlsec1-openssl -lxmlsec1 -lssl -lcrypto

Link order matters: xmlsec must come before openssl (symbols from xmlsec
resolve against openssl, not the other way around). GNU ld and lld are
order-sensitive for static archives, so reversing the order yields
undefined-symbol errors that look unrelated to the root cause.

Note: earlier releases of this SDK shipped ext/openssl/ with headers only
(no static libs). If you previously worked around that by pulling OpenSSL
from CocoaPods or building from source, you can now link directly against
the libs in ext/openssl/lib/iOS/ instead.
